---
// Reference / CLI / Global options.
//
// Written rather than generated: these flags are the shape of the binary, not
// a command in the catalog, so nothing in the release tarball describes them.
//
// It lives here as a page rather than in src/content/docs/reference/, because
// the presence of that directory is what switches generated-reference staging
// on in scripts/fetch-docs.mjs. Creating one file there stages 148 pages that
// collide with the routes under src/pages/docs/reference/.
//
// The output-format half is the part that matters most: 1.2.2 gave the default
// mode to people (strata-core issue 3306) and made --raw return bytes, and the
// site had nowhere that said so.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Global options"
  description="The flags every command accepts: which database to open, how durable a commit is, who else may open it, and which of the three output formats you get."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>Global options</h1>
  <p>
    These flags sit before the command and apply to all of them. <code
      >strata --json ./db kv get k</code
    > and <code>strata ./db --json kv get k</code> are the same line.
  </p>
  <h2>Choosing the database</h2>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata ./mydb kv get k
strata --db ./mydb kv get k
strata --cache kv get k</code></pre>
  <p>The positional path and <code>--db</code> are the same thing and cannot be combined.</p>
  <p>
    <code>--cache</code> opens an in-memory database instead: nothing is written to disk and nothing survives
    the process, which is what you want in a test.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>STRATA_DB</code> names a default path, so a shell that exports it can drop the argument entirely.
  </p>
  <h2>Output formats</h2>
  <p>Three formats, and the one you get when you ask for nothing is the one meant for a person.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>flag</th><th>for</th><th>shape</th></tr></thead><tbody
      ><tr
        ><td><code>--human</code></td><td>a reader</td><td>lines, tables and one-line receipts</td
        ></tr
      ><tr
        ><td><code>--json</code></td><td>a program</td><td
          >one compact JSON document, enveloped and typed</td
        ></tr
      ><tr
        ><td><code>--raw</code></td><td>a shell</td><td
          >bare values, tab-separated, no header or summary</td
        ></tr
      ></tbody
    >
  </table>
  <p>The same read in each:</p>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata ./db kv get greeting
strata --raw ./db kv get greeting
strata --json ./db kv get greeting</code></pre>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-out"><code>hello
hello
&#123;&quot;data&quot;:&#123;&quot;found&quot;:true,&quot;value&quot;:&#123;&quot;timestamp&quot;:3,&quot;value&quot;:&quot;aGVsbG8=&quot;,&quot;version&quot;:3&#125;&#125;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;kv_versioned_value&quot;&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    A list is where they diverge most. <code>--human</code> draws a table, <code>--raw</code> drops the
    header so <code>cut</code> and <code>awk</code> work:
  </p>
  <pre class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata ./db kv scan --limit 2</code></pre>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-out"><code>KEY  VERSION  VALUE
a          3  1
b          4  2</code></pre>
  <pre class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata --raw ./db kv scan --limit 2</code></pre>
  <pre class="not-prose gopt-out"><code>a	3	1
b	4	2</code></pre>
  <p>
    A write is a receipt in <code>--human</code>, its identity alone in <code>--raw</code>, and the
    full commit record in <code>--json</code>:
  </p>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata ./db kv put c 3
strata --raw ./db kv put d 4</code></pre>
  <pre class="not-prose gopt-out"><code>created c
d</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>--human</code> is worth naming explicitly in one case: a line inside a session that was opened
    with <code>--json</code> or <code>--raw</code> inherits that session's format, and
  </p>
  <p><code>--human</code> is how you get a reader's lines back for one command.</p>
  <p>
    <code>--json</code> is the stable contract. It is the format to parse, and the only one whose shape
    is promised across releases.
  </p>
  <h2>Values that are not text</h2>
  <p><code>--human</code> marks an encoded value rather than handing you something ambiguous:</p>
  <pre class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata ./db kv get raw</code></pre>
  <pre class="not-prose gopt-out"><code>base64:AAH/</code></pre>
  <p>
    The <code>base64:</code> prefix means a value that happens to read as <code>AAH/</code> cannot be
    confused with one that was encoded. For the bytes themselves, ask <code>--raw</code>, which
    writes them exactly and adds <strong>no trailing newline</strong>, so the file round trips back
    through <code>kv put --file</code>:
  </p>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata --raw ./db kv get raw &gt; payload.bin</code></pre>
  <p>
    Inside a session or a pipe each answer still ends with a line break. That separator belongs to
    the stream, not to the value.
  </p>
  <h2>Durability and sharing</h2>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata --durability always ./db kv put k v
strata --ipc off ./db kv get k
strata --read-only ./db kv get k</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>--durability</code> is <code>standard</code> by default, which acknowledges from a buffered
    write-ahead log and syncs at close or at a threshold. <code>always</code> syncs every commit before
    acknowledging it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--ipc</code> decides how a durable database is shared. <code>host</code>, the default,
    wins the lock and serves a socket other processes broker to; <code>client</code> brokers to an existing
    owner without hosting one; <code>off</code> opts out of both, for a single process with no socket.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--read-only</code> rejects every write-classified command, enforced by the owner's dispatch
    gate when brokered and at the connection otherwise.
  </p>
  <h2>Defaults for branch and space</h2>
  <pre
    class="not-prose gopt-console"><code>strata --branch work ./db kv get k
strata --space analytics ./db kv get k</code></pre>
  <p>
    Both set the default for commands that accept one, so they save repeating the flag. <a
      href="/docs/learn/branches">Branches</a
    > and <a href="/docs/learn/spaces">Spaces</a> cover what they select.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .gopt-console,
  .gopt-out {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: var(--text-mono-body, 0.9375rem);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .gopt-console {
    background: var(--color-inset);
    color: var(--color-ink-hi);
  }
  .gopt-out {
    background: var(--color-panel);
    color: var(--color-ink-mid);
  }
</style>
